            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for the world of work, then the Mandarin for someone under one at work."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 语文",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "语文 — yǔwén — \"Chinese (as a school subject)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我最喜欢语文课 wǒ zuì xǐhuan yǔwén kè — Chinese is my favourite lesson."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "语文 (yǔwén) is built from 语 'speech' and 文 'writing', both inherited from Middle Chinese, ngjoX and mjun (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'to discuss' and the second 'a written piece'. The name of the school subject is modern and not given there."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying how it was."
